      "content": "Wavefronts bunch up, leading to a higher frequency and a higher pitch!\n\n**Final Answer:** C $\checkmark\checkmark$\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $f_L = \\left( \\frac{v \\pm v_L}{v \\mp v_s} \\right) f_s$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $f_L$ is the frequency heard by the listener (in Hertz, $\\text{Hz}$)\n  * $v$ is the speed of sound in air (in $\\text{m}\\cdot\\text{s}^{-1}$)\n  * $v_s$ is the speed of the source (the train)\n  * $f_s$ is the actual frequency emitted by the source\n* **Why we use it:** When a source moves towards a stationary listener ($v_L = 0$), the denominator ($v - v_s$) becomes smaller. Dividing by a smaller number increases the overall value of $f_L$, which mathematically proves that frequency and pitch increase as the train approaches.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Remember the golden rule for Doppler effect: When the source and listener move *towards* each other, use the signs that make the calculated frequency *larger* (minus sign in the denominator)."
